<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sutherland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sutherland is a historic county in the far north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, now part of the Highland council area, a thinly peopled land of moors, mountains and lochs. Its Gaelic name, Cataibh, recalls the Cait of Pictish times, while the English name comes from the Norse 'southern land', as seen from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>. In the early 19th century the Sutherland estates carried out some of the harshest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/highland-clearances">Highland Clearances</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sutherland.jpg" alt="Sutherland" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Visitor from Wikishire. This particular view by Inops · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sutherland-Scotland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sutherland is a historic county, registration county and lieutenancy area, stretching from the Atlantic in the west to the Pentland Firth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> in the east. It is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s fifth-largest county by area but has a smaller population than a medium-sized Lowland town. The west and north coasts have very high sea cliffs and deep sea lochs, and Cape Wrath is the most north-westerly point in Scotland. Ben More Assynt, at 998 metres, is the tallest peak in the county, and in the west isolated sandstone peaks such as Foinaven, Arkle and Suilven rise from Lewisian gneiss.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Much of the area belonged to the Pictish kingdom of Cat, which also included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>. In the 9th century it was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigurd-eysteinsson">Sigurd Eysteinsson</a>, Jarl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a>, and the Scottish crown claimed overlordship from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1098. The Diocese of Caithness was established in the 12th century, and its seat moved to Dornoch Cathedral, begun in 1224. About then an earldom of Sutherland was made from the southern part of the earldom of Orkney and Caithness. A shire called Sutherland was created in 1633, with Dornoch as head burgh, and the county ceased to be used for local government in 1975.</p><p>The Sutherland Estate, about two thirds of the county, saw the largest clearances in the Highlands, mostly in 1812, 1814 and 1819-20. In the last of these, 1,068 families, an estimated 5,400 people, were evicted. They were offered resettlement on the coast with work in fishing, but many moved to farms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a> or emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>.</p><p>The clearances concentrated Gaelic speakers in the new fishing villages and so prolonged the language there. East Sutherland Gaelic, spoken around Golspie, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brora">Brora</a> and Embo, was the last Gaelic dialect commonly spoken on the east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and its last known native speaker died in 2020.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The population peaked at 25,793 in 1851 and fell to 12,803 by the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Four of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s National Scenic Areas lie wholly or partly in the county. The pre-1975 boundaries are still used for land registration. A flag adopted in 2018 shows an overlapping saltire and Nordic cross, for Scottish and Norwegian heritage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sutherland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Goldman and Sally <b>Sutherland</b> Goldman later placed it around the middle of the first millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/work/ramayana">Ramayana · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>John <b>Sutherland</b> and Stephen Fender note that the choice embarrassed Britain's Labour government, whose leaders now had to stand for a melody tied to Rhodesia at European functions.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rise-o-voices-of-rhodesia">Rise, O Voices of Rhodesia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Caereni appear in Ptolemy's Geography of about 150 on the north-western mainland of Scotland, north of the Carnonacae, usually located in western <b>Sutherland</b> around Assynt and Kinlochbervie.</blockquote><a href="/state/caereni">Caereni</a></li><li><blockquote>The name, perhaps 'cat people', survives in Caithness, the Norse 'headland of the Cats', and Gaelic Cataibh, the name for <b>Sutherland</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/cait">Cait</a></li><li><blockquote>The Lugi are known only from Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, which places them in the far north of Scotland, usually located on the east coast of <b>Sutherland</b> around the Dornoch Firth and the Brora area, between the Decantae and the Cornavii (Caithness).</blockquote><a href="/state/lugi">Lugi</a></li><li><blockquote>The Smertae are known solely from Ptolemy's Geography of about 150, which places them inland in the far north of Scotland, usually located in central <b>Sutherland</b> around the upper Oykel and Shin valleys.</blockquote><a href="/state/smertae">Smertae</a></li></ul></section>
